            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-47265",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "python-aiohttp: AIOHTTP: Information disclosure via improper handling of cookies during cross-origin redirects"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "AIOHTTP is an asynchronous HTTP client/server framework for asyncio and Python. Prior to version 3.14.0, cookies set with the `cookies` parameter on requests are sent after following a cross-origin redirect. If a developer uses the `cookies` parameter on a per-request basis then sensitive data might be leaked to an attacker if they manage to control a redirect. Version 3.14.0 patches the issue. If unable to upgrade, using a `Cookie` header in the `headers` parameter is not vulnerable."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-47265",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-47265\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: aiohttp\nFixed Version: 3.14.0\nLink: [CVE-2026-47265](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-47265)\nAIOHTTP is an asynchronous HTTP client/server framework for asyncio and Python. Prior to version 3.14.0, cookies set with the `cookies` parameter on requests are sent after following a cross-origin redirect. If a developer uses the `cookies` parameter on a per-request basis then sensitive data might be leaked to an attacker if they manage to control a redirect. Version 3.14.0 patches the issue. If unable to upgrade, using a `Cookie` header in the `headers` parameter is not vulnerable.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-47265**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|aiohttp|3.14.0|[CVE-2026-47265](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-47265)|\n\nAIOHTTP is an asynchronous HTTP client/server framework for asyncio and Python. Prior to version 3.14.0, cookies set with the `cookies` parameter on requests are sent after following a cross-origin redirect. If a developer uses the `cookies` parameter on a per-request basis then sensitive data might be leaked to an attacker if they manage to control a redirect. Version 3.14.0 patches the issue. If unable to upgrade, using a `Cookie` header in the `headers` parameter is not vulnerable."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv40_baseScore": 6.6,
                "cvssv40_vector": "CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:N/VC:H/VI:N/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N/E:U",
                "precision": "very-high",
